AB  - Context:
AI systems increasingly support requirements, coding, testing, security review, release, and project coordination. Existing AI-governance guidance is useful, but often too general to specify how much authority AI should have in concrete software engineering decisions.
Objective:
This study develops and evaluates SE-AIGov, a governance matrix that links SE decisions to AI autonomy, human approval, audit needs, escalation triggers, reversibility, and risk.
Method:
We derived a 35-row candidate matrix from AI-governance, software-engineering, secure- development, DevOps/change-governance, human–AI oversight, and LLM-security sources. It was refined through a survey of 73 respondents, blind scenario review by eight role-specialized experts, a neutral re-check of domain-sensitive scenarios, and evaluation by six additional domain-aware experts.
Results:
The process produced an 18-row matrix and a domain-sensitive escalation overlay for healthcare/ safety-critical software, regulated finance, education/minor data, public-sector accessibility and language inclusion, critical infrastructure, and legal/procurement commitments. Expert judgments showed strongest agreement on risk, reversibility, approval, and audit requirements. Autonomy required more conditional wording, especially at the transition from review and recommendation to human-authorized execution.
Conclusions:
The evidence supports SE-AIGov as an understandable artifact for formative review and structured governance discussion. Respondents and experts could apply the matrix and identify cases needing stricter rules. The study does not establish that its autonomy levels are objectively correct or that SE-AIGov improves organizational outcomes. Field evaluation is still needed.
