AB  - Vector hubness, the tendency of a few points to be nearest neighbours of disproportionately many queries in high-dimensional space, is an attack surface for retrieval-augmented generation: one crafted document can be retrieved for many unrelated queries. The prevailing defence is periodic detection, which leaves an exposure window and requires corpus-wide rescans. We study admission-time control, scoring each candidate against sentinel queries and refusing it before it becomes retrievable, and ask whether that control can be maintained incrementally. Two results follow. First, a single global gate suffices. On a 100,000-document BEIR corpus, with attacker anchors disjoint from the defender’s sentinels, it recovers effective adversarial hubs at recall 1.0 (AUROC ≈1.0) at an ≈1% false-positive rate on in-domain general documents, and averages 0.91 ± 0.07 recall on gradient-realised attacks. Second, refining the gate to be domain-aware adds nothing: the per-topic marginal over global is ＋0.000 across five encoders, two corpora and two concept definitions. We give a geometric account: anisotropic embeddings couple topic-local and global visibility, so a hub cannot be topic-loud yet globally quiet. The thresholds are maintained incrementally, exactly and at a per-write cost independent of corpus size. On a real HNSW index admission adds ≈3.1% to ingestion latency, its scoring cost is flat to N=106, and 1.2% of admit/quarantine decisions flip under approximate indexing, none of them attacks; results reproduce on x86 server hardware. We advocate a preventive global admission gate, with provenance as a complementary control for the tight-domain residual.
