AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are an important paradigm for natural-language pattern recognition, yet their safety and trustworthiness remain key bottlenecks for deployment in high-risk scenarios. In-context learning (ICL) is a core approach for adapting LLMs to downstream pattern recognition tasks, while latent context injection improves efficiency by encoding demonstrations into compact latent representations rather than concatenating full demonstrations during inference. However, this strategy also introduces a trustworthiness risk: over-amplified contextual priors may distort query-conditioned semantics, causing outputs to deviate from input semantic evidence and undermining the output reliability and semantic robustness of pattern recognition systems. We further show that such context-induced semantic drift is inherently sample-dependent, indicating that uniform regularization is insufficient. We propose UAD-ICL, an uncertainty-aware semantic control framework for trustworthy latent context-based ICL, designed to mitigate semantic drift risk. UAD-ICL treats latent context injection as a sample-wise control problem. During training, it leverages the uncertainty of the query-only distribution to adaptively weight semantic alignment across samples, thereby achieving a better balance between task guidance and semantic fidelity and mitigating the trustworthiness risk caused by semantic drift. Experiments across multiple datasets and backbone LLMs show consistent gains over latent context injection methods. UAD-ICL surpasses conventional few-shot ICL on several datasets, enhances semantic robustness and output reliability, and preserves the efficiency advantages of latent context methods.
