AB  - Large Language Model (LLM) agents are increasingly used for repository-level software tasks such as reading project documents, inspecting source code, invoking tools, and producing candidate patches. In these settings, producing a functionally correct change is not enough. The process must also respect project rules, permission boundaries, and the need for an auditable link between the final action and the evidence used to justify it. This paper presents CARET - a Spec-Driven Development (SDD) framework for governed agentic code generation in repository editing workflows. Its central mechanism is a machine-readable Architecture Card that states what constraints the patch must satisfy, what runtime checks must pass, what tool actions are permitted in a given environment, and what evidence must appear in the execution trace before sensitive actions are allowed. The framework combines this specification with mediated tool access, structured trace logging, a verification gate, and bounded repair after rejection. Evaluation on CARET-Bench, an open-source benchmark for governance-sensitive repository editing, shows that the approach adds only a little cost on routine tasks while improving the rate of successful changes that also satisfy governance requirements on more demanding tasks. The results also show that governance checking alone is not sufficient; effective improvement also requires targeted repair support after rejection.
