AB  - Modern distributed edge computing systems face escalating challenges in resource allocation and task scheduling across geographically dispersed, computationally heterogeneous nodes while satisfying diverse quality-of-service requirements and adapting to dynamic network conditions. Traditional optimization approaches, operating on rigid mathematical formulations, fail to capture the semantic complexity and contextual nuances inherent in contemporary distributed workloads where tasks carry implicit requirements expressed through natural language specifications. We present a novel semantic-aware orchestration framework that interprets task semantics, reasons about resource trade-offs, and generates intelligent scheduling decisions through natural language understanding. Our hierarchical architecture integrates global semantic reasoning for network-wide coordination with localized controllers for real-time regional execution. The LLM acts as a semantic policy generator that outputs relaxed placement scores and natural-language directives, whereas feasibility with respect to hard constraints is enforced by a deterministic ADMM projection. By embedding task descriptions into semantic vector spaces and employing retrieval-augmented decision making, the system achieves intelligent task co-location, minimizes data transfer overhead, and naturally enforces constraints expressed in natural language. Comprehensive evaluation on a 500-node distributed testbed processing 60,000 heterogeneous tasks demonstrates 37% reduction in average completion time, 42% improvement in resource utilization efficiency, and 28% decrease in deadline violations compared to state-of-the-art baselines including reinforcement learning and graph neural network approaches. When all learned baselines are re-tuned under matched training budgets and given the same semantic embeddings, the completion-time reduction against the strongest baseline remains 23.3%. Production deployment in distributed medical imaging infrastructure over 30 days indicates practical viability in this setting, with zero constraint violations and an 82% reduction in manual interventions, and points to semantic understanding as a promising direction for autonomous resource management in large-scale distributed computing environments, subject to the cost, privacy and availability conditions analysed in the paper.
