AB  - This systematic literature review examines the emerging field of Federated Learning for Insider Threat Detection (FL-ITD). Following PRISMA guidelines, 20 peer-reviewed studies published between 2016 and 2025 were analyzed to assess datasets, aggregation strategies, machine learning architectures, privacy-preserving mechanisms, and evaluation practices in FL-based ITD. The review further introduces a unified taxonomy and a multi-dimensional evaluation framework for realistic federated assessment. The findings show that, despite the growing maturity of FL in intrusion detection, its application to insider threat detection remains fragmented and constrained by unrealistic datasets, simplified federated settings, severe non-IID distributions, behavioral heterogeneity, and inconsistent evaluation methodologies. Additional challenges related to privacy preservation, adversarial robustness, communication efficiency, and explainability further limit operational applicability. Emerging directions such as multimodal behavioral AI, personalized federated learning, explainable federated analytics, and large language model–based semantic reasoning show strong potential for more adaptive and context-aware insider threat detection. Overall, this review identifies the main structural limitations of current FL-ITD research and outlines key directions toward scalable, trustworthy, and deployment-ready systems.
