AB  - Next-generation Security Operations Centers (SOCs) are increasingly adopting Artificial Intelligence (AI) to address the persistent “detection deficit” and mitigate the problem of alert fatigue associated with traditional signature-based security systems. This Systematic Literature Review (SLR) evaluates the integration of AI-driven Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) and Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR) platforms as enablers of proactive cyber defense. Following the three-phase SLR methodology proposed by Kitchenham and Charters, this review analyzes 50 primary studies published between 2015 and 2026, covering data sources that include enterprise logs, Open-Source Intelligence (OSINT), and synthetic benchmarks. The findings reveal a clear shift toward context-aware architectures that leverage MITRE ATT&CK mappings and causal graph analytics to reconstruct multi-stage attack scenarios. Several studies report detection rates exceeding 95% under controlled experimental conditions; however, performance generalization to real-world production environments remains a critical open challenge. Emerging trends highlight Bayesian uncertainty quantification and synthetic data augmentation as promising strategies for addressing the scarcity of labeled real-world security telemetry. Despite these advances, significant research gaps persist, including the limited explainability of deep learning models used in high-stakes decision contexts, insufficient study of the long-term effects of automation on analyst cognitive load, and a lack of rigorous adversarial robustness evaluation. This review concludes by proposing a comprehensive research roadmap for transitioning toward autonomous, human-centered security operations.
