AB  - AI applications are rapidly improving from productivity enhancing tools identifying information to semi-autonomous/autonomous applications that can complete multi-step processes. While the ability of AI to complete multi-step processes may enable greater levels of work automation, technology enabled autonomous decision making also represents potential risks. In this paper we propose a framework for organizations and researchers regarding mechanisms to establishing adequate governance oversight of Agentic AI applications. The framework combines a structured use-case intake process, risk tiering, explicit autonomy levels, cybersecurity review, responsible-use controls, pilot requirements, and recertification. We argue that the appropriate degree of human oversight should reflect both the autonomy of the system and the organizational risk of the business process. Low-risk administrative support may justify light-touch oversight, whereas workflows that affect patient access, billing, or enterprise records require formal approval, logging, escalation, and continuous monitoring. The result is a practical, enterprise-oriented model intended to help hospitals and researchers move toward a more mature, consistent, and evidence-aligned way of evaluating agentic AI.
