AB  - Data sharing is a critical pathway for unlocking the value of data assets, yet it faces significant privacy risks. Differential Privacy (DP), with its rigorous mathematical guarantees, has become a pivotal technology for balancing data utility and privacy protection. This paper systematically reviews the evolution of DP in the context of data sharing, tracing its technological development. Building on theoretical frameworks and applications in typical data-sharing scenarios, we propose an innovative model that shifts the privacy value from "sample sharing" to "knowledge sharing." In response to the unique challenges posed by large models, particularly memory-related threats, we develop a comprehensive, generative AI-based lifecycle defense architecture that encompasses model training, interactive inference, and data flow. This paper further explores the intrinsic logic behind the evolution of privacy protection technologies, from early gradient constraints to parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) and agent-based system-level defenses. We also examine the academic and industrial progress in extending these technologies from general infrastructure to cutting-edge generative AI applications. Finally, we outline the current challenges and propose future research directions.
