AB  - Large language models and modern text classifiers remain vulnerable to adversarial text perturbations that preserve human interpretability while deceiving automated systems. In this work we study precise language deception: targeted adversarial examples guided by explainable AI (XAI) rankings under restricted knowledge of the model. Building on our earlier conference paper (Gniewkowski et al., 2025), where we used global importance rankings derived from an XAI subset to construct highly targeted word-level attacks, this extended journal version significantly broadens the experimental and methodological scope. First, we introduce a mitigation strategy in which the classifier is retrained on adversarially perturbed training samples generated from XAI-based rankings. We quantify how such adversarial data augmentation affects robustness to targeted attacks. Second, we analyze robustness under potential distribution drift by leveraging generative models for automatic paraphrasing: adversarial examples that successfully bypass the classifier are paraphrased, and we evaluate the resulting performance of both a BERT-based classifier and GPT-4o-mini on these paraphrased variants. Third, we systematically investigate how well XAI-derived global importance rankings generalize beyond the XAI subset by comparing them with rankings recomputed on the attacked test split. This allows us to assess the transferability and stability of XAI-guided attack strategies. Overall, our results provide a more complete picture of the interaction between XAI, targeted adversarial attacks, and robustness-improving mitigation, highlighting both the power and the limitations of using explanation-derived rankings as a tool for auditing and hardening language models.
