AB  - AI search is increasingly becoming an interface through which consumers discover and compare products, and the retrieval layer supplying these systems is open to paid manipulation that carries no disclosure. This study terms such manipulation commercial poisoning and assesses it across three major providers (Google, OpenAI, Anthropic) in controlled experiments comprising 1800 observations. Six economy-tier models were evaluated over two cohorts using a synthetic corpus of consumer product content with contamination varied from 0 to 5%. Economy-tier models prove highly susceptible: 1% contamination produces bias rates of 50%–100%, and susceptibility rises sharply with query specificity, peaking on the product comparisons consumers rely on most. Contamination effects are governed by a threshold rather than a dose, so minimal investment secures near-maximal influence. Contextual poisoning circumvents explicit neutrality instructions in RLHF models at rates of 47.50% to 66.38%, against 4.17% under Constitutional AI (Cohen’s h=1.11 to 1.49). Testimonial content consistently outperforms fabricated statistics in this retrieval setting, supporting the narrative-transportation account under AI-mediated product discovery. The proposed Commercial Poisoning Framework attributes these vulnerabilities to attacker economics that make manipulation self-financing, provider underinvestment arising from externalized security benefits, and the asymmetry between per-campaign attack and per-query defence. The findings identify commercial poisoning as undisclosed advertising that circumvents the separation between paid placement and organic content on which consumer discounting depends, distorts competition in favour of firms investing in retrieval optimization irrespective of product quality, and concentrates exposure among price-sensitive consumers and merchants.
