AB  - Coordinating populations of LLM-powered agents toward cooperative outcomes while preserving ethical guarantees is a central challenge for intelligent generative systems deployed in adversarial environments. We present a framework in which a central LLM coordinator compiles natural-language directives into structured influence policies that are filtered by a constitutional governance layer before delivery to a heterogeneous population of grounded agents. Each agent follows a hybrid decision model that combines game-theoretic base probabilities with LLM-evaluated narrative shifts attenuated by per-agent resistance, and is instantiated from one of six personality archetypes (pragmatist, idealist, skeptic, conformist, strategist, opportunist) that reflect the diversity of real-world decision-maker profiles. Four experiments on a Barabási–Albert scale-free network of 30 agents over 50 time steps, powered by Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct, demonstrate that constitutional governance reliably preserves ethical coordination quality under full adversarial pressure: governed agents achieve an Ethical Cooperation Score (ECS) of 0.176 versus an unconstrained baseline of ECS=0, enforced through hard constraints on factual claim integrity (1.000 vs. 0.000). We identify an autonomy paradox in which unconstrained agents exhibit higher measured autonomy (0.856 vs. 0.728) by resisting manipulation more forcefully; yet, their overall coordination quality collapses through integrity failure, showing that agent-level resistance alone cannot substitute for system-level governance. Critically, the ECS governance advantage grows monotonically with mean resistance (+0.174 at R=0.10 to +0.183 at R=0.70), revealing that governance filtering and agent autonomy are complementary rather than redundant mechanisms in LLM-mediated coordination. Multi-seed bootstrap validation (Cohen’s d=8.68 for ECS) and an extended robustness sweep across three network topologies and five LLM backbones confirm that the governance advantage is structural rather than backbone- or seed-specific. These findings carry direct implications for the design of trustworthy LLM-driven autonomous agents and workflow automation systems operating in complex, adversarially contested environments.
