AB  - With the extensive application of Large Language Models (LLMs) in natural language processing, content generation, and human-computer interaction, semantic security and controllability issues have become increasingly prominent. Semantic security concerns not only privacy and data protection but also the accuracy, reliability, and ethical compliance of generated content. However, LLMs often exhibit problems such as semantic divergence, irrelevant answers, and hallucinations in practical applications, making it difficult to guarantee the accuracy and controllability of semantic transmission. To address these challenges, we propose a semantic security framework oriented towards DIKWP (Data, Information, Knowledge, Wisdom, and Purpose) artificial consciousness, termed 3No-SemMath-DIKWPAC, designed to enhance the semantic security of LLMs in external user input and output. By introducing the DIKWP model of semantic mathematics, we construct a theoretical foundation covering four-space (concept, semantic, cognitive, and consciousness space), achieving full-process semantic modeling and analysis of a web-like pattern of data, information, knowledge, wisdom, and purpose. Our framework comprises two core modules: 3-No problem (incomplete, imprecise, and inconsistent) handling and semantic detection processing, designed to address semantic ambiguity and insecurity in human-computer interactions. In 3-No problem handling, we propose a purpose-driven DIKWP fusion transformation method that dynamically generates and adjusts semantics to enhance understanding and overcome the limitations of traditional methods under the open-world assumption. Furthermore, by constructing an unsafe sentence database and conducting explicit and implicit toxicity detection, we enhance the safety and compliance of generated resources. Experimental results show that our proposed framework performs better in external user input-output semantic security and controllability. Future work includes optimizing the framework and extending it to multimodal resources and dynamically updated toxicity repositories.
