AB  - The rapid advancement of deep generative models has dramatically escalated the threat posed by malicious deepfakes, with face manipulation presenting significant risks to identity security, privacy, and societal trust. Despite the growing number of disruption-based defense methods, a systematic understanding of their underlying mechanisms, evaluation practices, and limitations remains lacking. This survey provides a comprehensive review of proactive deepfake disruption techniques designed to prevent or degrade malicious face manipulation. First, we introduce a unified taxonomy that categorizes existing approaches into adversarial perturbation, data poisoning, and latent-space manipulation methods. Second, we critically examine evaluation protocols and metrics for measuring attack effectiveness, perceptual quality, and robustness. Third, we review widely used benchmark datasets and analyze their evolution alongside advances in generative models. Finally, we discuss key challenges, including resilience against image purification techniques, cross-model generalization, and deployment scalability, while highlighting promising directions for future research. The insights provided in this survey aim to support the development of more effective and robust defenses against malicious deepfake generation.
