AB  - Corrosion-induced leakage accidents in refinery and petrochemical facilities are characterized by complex escalation processes and stringent response constraints. Static text-based emergency plans are difficult to rapidly transform into executable procedures, while general-purpose large language models may generate hallucinated or infeasible instructions because of insufficient awareness of engineering constraints and changing operational states. To address these challenges, this study proposes R-Flow, a trustworthy emergency procedure generation framework comprising three modules: R-FlowIEKG, R-FlowGen, and R-FlowLoop. These modules respectively support process-element extraction and process-centric knowledge graph construction, hard-constraint-enhanced procedure generation, and operational-evidence-driven closed-loop correction. Using 9,200 manually annotated samples, document-level five-fold cross-validation yielded an average F1-score of 93.8%. Under the unified comparative protocol, the best R-FlowGen configuration achieved a compliance generation rate of 95.4% with an end-to-end response latency of approximately 7.62 s. For closed-loop correction, R-FlowLoop achieved conflict interception rates of 93.3%, 85.7%, and 30.0% in fault-injected, naturally occurring, and DCS/SCADA alarm-event scenarios, respectively, increasing the overall rate from 5.6% to 79.6%. Three representative case studies further show that R-Flow can correct infeasible response paths, adapt procedures to changing operational evidence, and interrupt representative escalation pathways within critical response windows. The results demonstrate a feasible approach for constraint-aware and evidence-driven intelligent emergency decision support in refinery corrosion scenarios.
