AB  - Modern malware detection systems increasingly rely on machine learning models to identify evolving threats. However, they suffer from limited dataset size and generality. The scarcity of large datasets stems from organizations’ reluctance to share their data, as this would result in concrete advantages to competitors. Furthermore, sensitive data sharing would be risky in terms of potency, as it could disclose defensive strategies and threat intelligence capabilities. The limited size and specificity of local datasets reduce the generalization of detection models across diverse threat landscapes. While centralized approaches improve performance through data pooling, they require sacrificing privacy and competitive advantages. Federated Learning (FL) enables multiple organizations to collaboratively train machine learning models without sharing raw data, allowing participants to leverage collective intelligence while preserving data sovereignty. FL provides access to broader patterns than a single organization alone. However, the challenge of a limited local dataset at each participant persists. This paper evaluates the suitability of Generative Adversarial Networks and Large Language Models for synthetic malware data augmentation. We evaluate their effectiveness across tabular and image-based malware datasets under varying federation scales and augmentation levels. Among the evaluated generative approaches, GAN augmentation improved accuracy by up to 4.8 and 17.3 percentage points on tabular and image data, respectively. The 34.2% ratio was the only GAN setting that consistently outperformed the baseline across both datasets, federation scales, and communication-round settings; lower ratios were configuration-dependent and sometimes reduced accuracy.
