AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) suffer from hallucination, a systemic weakness that erodes trust by producing confident but factually incorrect content, especially in high-stakes settings. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) directly addresses this limitation by tightly coupling generation to verifiable, up-to-date evidence. This comprehensive survey introduces a unified four-stage taxonomy of Indexing, Retrieval, Fusion, and Generation that deconstructs modern RAG architectures and provides a common lens for comparing systems. Drawing on more than 300 references, we analyze the full spectrum from foundational Vector RAG to the multi-hop reasoning capabilities of Graph RAG, and we chart emerging paradigms such as Agentic RAG, Multimodal RAG, Hybrid RAG, and reasoning-centric variants. Beyond architectural taxonomies, we provide a deep implementation-oriented treatment of indexing and similarity search, including a practical comparison of leading vector databases and graph databases for real-world deployments. We further present a comprehensive benchmark comparison that unifies evaluation datasets and metrics for both vector- and graph-based pipelines, and we consolidate trade-offs across representative RAG strategies in a comparison of key approaches. Together, these contributions position RAG as a mature design pattern for building grounded, auditable AI systems and offer practitioners, graduate students, and researchers a structured roadmap from basic pipelines to advanced, evidence-based reasoning systems.
