AB  - Renewable and hydrogen-based energy systems are increasingly complex to operate and coordinate, yet most artificial intelligence applied to them consists of task-specific predictors and controllers. This review examines agentic artificial intelligence (AGAI), goal-directed agents that plan, coordinate, and adapt, for the management of renewable–hydrogen systems. It proposes five criteria that separate AGAI from classical multi-agent systems; a reference formulation of a multi-agent coordination framework as a decentralized partially observable decision process with consensus-based and contract-net coordination; five use cases with literature evidence; a human-in-the-loop safety layer with a run-time safety filter and re-certification after continual learning; an evaluation framework with benchmark scenarios and metrics; and a guideline for selecting centralized, hierarchical, or decentralized topologies. An illustrative simulation of a photovoltaic–wind–hydrogen microgrid on public 2019 data compares rule-based dispatch, model predictive control, a perfect-foresight benchmark, and hierarchical agents supervised by rules or by a locally served 3-billion-parameter language model. With stored hydrogen valued at its replacement cost, model predictive control came within 7% of the perfect-foresight cost and served all servable load during 24 h of islanding. The supervisors reduced its residual load shedding in a low-renewable period from 0.24 to 0.03 MWh or less but raised costs by up to 34% in ordinary weeks. The language model returned no invalid output in 888 calls, with a mean response time of 5.1 s, but its decisions varied little with conditions. Deployment barriers and a research agenda are discussed, and AGAI should be evaluated against correctly specified conventional baselines.
ER  - 

