UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1566253526003568
KW  - Persona modeling
KW  - Prompt engineering
KW  - Synthetic fine-tuning
KW  - Generalization saturation
KW  - Reasoning divergence
AB  - Role-enacted agents based on Large Language Models are increasingly used in computational social science to support social simulation, opinion modeling, and bias analysis. However, the two main approaches for constructing such personas, namely prompt engineering and synthetic fine-tuning, present a clear trade-off between achieving consistent persona behavior and maintaining stable model performance. Prompt engineering generally preserves the model’s reasoning ability but is limited in producing emotionally intense or extreme personas because of built-in safety constraints. Synthetic fine-tuning enhances persona consistency but often reduces generalization and introduces shifts in reasoning patterns. To study these effects, this work introduces an information-theoretic framework that evaluates how persona conditioning alters internal model behavior. Two metrics are proposed: Generalization Saturation, which identifies the upper limit of persona information a model can absorb, and Reasoning Divergence, which measures changes in reasoning performance after persona conditioning. Experiments show that synthetic fine-tuning reaches a saturation point where additional persona information no longer improves memory and is associated with measurable shifts in reasoning behavior, while prompt-based conditioning is associated with unintended performance changes on unrelated tasks, with fluctuations that can exceed 30%. A multi-turn anti-hate dialogue simulation further demonstrates that the proposed metrics capture behavioral escalation patterns and reasoning stability in high-risk scenarios. The framework provides practical tools for assessing cognitive risks and for developing robust and reliable persona agents for social simulation applications.
ER  - 

