SN  - 0167-4048
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cose.2026.105236
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167404826004128
KW  - Software vulnerability discovery
KW  - Static analysis
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Program analysis
KW  - Java web security
AB  - Static application security testing (SAST) can cheaply generate high-recall candidate alerts, but auditing those alerts remains a primary bottleneck in large-scale vulnerability discovery. Recent LLM-assisted workflows mostly treat the model as a post hoc verifier. This passive verification design helps filter scanner outputs, but it still inherits the search boundary of static tools and fails to reuse the semantic information revealed by confirmed true and false positives. We present vEcho, an evidence-grounded vulnerability auditing framework for Java web applications that turns alert triage into a proactive discovery process. vEcho keeps static tools as high-recall candidate producers, uses ScanAgent to search repository regions where static analysis is likely to miss framework behavior, indirect propagation, semantic validation, or business logic, and uses VerifyAgent to judge exploitability from repository-grounded evidence. At the core of the feedback loop, vEcho introduces Echoic Vulnerability Propagation (EVP) to turn confirmed outcomes into reusable guidance for further search. Evaluations on CWE-Bench-Java demonstrate that vEcho detects 73 out of 120 benchmark vulnerabilities (60.83%), compared with 50 out of 120 (41.67%) for IRIS, and improves AvgF1 from 0.1166 to 0.3974. Stage-wise analysis validates this closed-loop design. Raw static findings account for 29 detections, adding ScanAgent and VerifyAgent raises this to 59, and EVP-guided re-scanning lifts the total to 73. Beyond the benchmark-matched detections, vEcho surfaces 24 additional confirmed vulnerabilities within the corpus. In real-world audits across 21 open-source projects, system-produced vEcho reports led to 52 responsibly disclosed vulnerabilities, yielding 25 assigned CVE IDs.
ER  - 

